# 5. Counseling Skills and Interventions (NCE content outline, 30%): 60 items
# Theories, microskills, crisis, family systems and group work.
CAT = "nceSkills"; SEG = "SKL"
Q = [
# Microskills
("A client says, \"My boss criticized me in front of everyone.\" Which response is a paraphrase?",
 "\"Your boss called you out publicly.\"", ["\"How did that make you feel?\"", "\"You should go and talk to HR.\"", "\"That happened to me once, too.\""],
 "A paraphrase restates the content of the client's message in fresh words. Asking about feelings is a question, and the others are advice and self-disclosure."),
("Which counselor question is open-ended?",
 "\"What happened after the argument?\"", ["\"Did you two argue again later that day?\"", "\"Was the argument on Monday?\"", "\"Are you still upset about it?\""],
 "Open questions invite the client to elaborate and usually begin with what or how. Closed questions can be answered with yes, no or a fact."),
("A counselor says, \"You say you're fine, yet you've missed work all week.\" This skill is:",
 "Confrontation", ["Reflection of feeling", "Summarizing", "Minimal encouragement"],
 "Confrontation points out a discrepancy between what the client says and does, gently and supportively, to promote awareness."),
("A counselor pulls together the main themes from the session before it ends. This skill is:",
 "Summarizing", ["Paraphrasing", "Probing", "Interpreting"],
 "Summarizing ties together several statements or themes, often at the start or end of a session or when changing focus."),
("A counselor says, \"Right now, as you talk about her, I notice you're clenching your fists.\" This skill is:",
 "Immediacy", ["Self-disclosure", "Reframing", "Advice giving"],
 "Immediacy addresses what is happening between counselor and client, or in the client, in the present moment."),
("A client calls herself stubborn. The counselor suggests she is determined. This is:",
 "Reframing", ["Confronting", "Paraphrasing", "Probing"],
 "Reframing offers a new, often more positive, way of seeing the same behavior or situation, which can open new options."),
("A counselor in long-term recovery is asked by a client if she has ever had a drinking problem. What should guide her answer?",
 "Whether it serves the client's goals", ["Whether she feels like sharing", "Whether the client seems curious", "Whether the session is nearly over"],
 "Self-disclosure is used sparingly, briefly and only when it serves the client, after which the focus returns to the client. The counselor's own wish to share is not a reason."),
("A client pauses for a long time after a painful statement. What is usually the most helpful counselor response?",
 "Allow the silence for a moment", ["Change to a lighter topic", "Ask several quick questions", "Fill the gap with some advice"],
 "Silence gives the client time to reflect and feel. Rushing to fill it can cut off important processing."),
# Theories
("A counselor helps a client find the irrational belief behind her distress and dispute it. This approach is:",
 "Rational emotive behavior therapy", ["Person-centered therapy", "Gestalt experiential dream therapy", "Classical psychoanalysis"],
 "Ellis's REBT uses the ABCDE model: an activating event, beliefs, consequences, disputing irrational beliefs and an effective new belief."),
("In the ABC model of REBT, what is \"B\"?",
 "The belief about the event", ["The behavior that follows", "The body's stress response", "The background history"],
 "A is the activating event, B is the belief about it and C is the emotional and behavioral consequence. Ellis argued B, not A, causes C."),
("A client thinks, \"I failed one quiz, so I'm a total failure.\" In Beck's cognitive therapy, this is:",
 "Overgeneralization", ["Personalization", "Mind reading", "Emotional reasoning"],
 "Overgeneralization draws a broad conclusion from a single event. Personalization is taking responsibility for events outside one's control."),
("A cognitive therapist asks, \"What evidence supports that thought, and what evidence goes against it?\" This technique is:",
 "Socratic questioning", ["Free association", "Empty chair work", "Paradoxical intention"],
 "Socratic questioning guides clients to test their own automatic thoughts against the evidence rather than being told they are wrong."),
("A client with a fear of elevators learns relaxation, then imagines steps from least to most feared. This is:",
 "Systematic desensitization", ["Flooding in imagination", "Aversive conditioning", "Token economy"],
 "Wolpe's systematic desensitization pairs relaxation with a graded hierarchy of feared situations. Flooding exposes the client to the most feared situation at once."),
("A counselor uses a client's own pace and rarely directs the session, trusting the client's capacity to grow. This approach is:",
 "Person-centered", ["Reality therapy", "Behavioral", "Strategic family therapy"],
 "Rogers's person-centered approach is nondirective and rests on the core conditions of congruence, unconditional positive regard and empathy."),
("A counselor asks a client to speak to an empty chair as if her late mother were in it. This approach is:",
 "Gestalt therapy", ["Adlerian therapy", "REBT", "Solution-focused therapy"],
 "The empty chair is a Gestalt technique for working through unfinished business in the here and now."),
("A counselor explores a client's birth order and early recollections to understand his lifestyle. This approach is:",
 "Adlerian", ["Existential", "Narrative", "Behavioral"],
 "Adlerian counselors assess lifestyle through family constellation, birth order and early recollections, and value social interest."),
("An Adlerian counselor asks a shy client to behave for a week as if she were confident. This technique is:",
 "Acting as if", ["Spitting in the soup", "Paradoxical intention", "Exception finding"],
 "Acting as if invites clients to try out the behavior they want, which can change how they see themselves."),
("A reality therapist asks a client, \"Is what you are doing getting you what you want?\" This follows which model?",
 "WDEP", ["ABCDE", "SOLER", "OARS"],
 "Glasser's reality therapy uses WDEP: wants, doing and direction, self-evaluation and planning. The question asks the client to evaluate current behavior."),
("A counselor asks, \"Suppose tonight a miracle happened and your problem was solved. What would be different tomorrow?\" This approach is:",
 "Solution-focused brief therapy", ["Psychoanalytic therapy", "Gestalt therapy", "Bowen multigenerational family therapy"],
 "The miracle question comes from solution-focused brief therapy, developed by de Shazer and Berg. It helps clients describe goals and possible solutions."),
("A solution-focused counselor asks, \"When was a time the problem didn't happen?\" This looks for:",
 "Exceptions", ["Defense mechanisms", "Irrational beliefs", "Triangles"],
 "Exception questions find times the problem was absent or less severe, so the client can do more of what worked."),
("A narrative counselor speaks of \"the depression\" as something that visits the client. This technique is:",
 "Externalizing the problem", ["Interpreting resistance", "Disputing beliefs", "Systematic desensitization"],
 "Externalizing separates the person from the problem, so the client can examine and resist it rather than see it as part of who they are."),
("An existential counselor would focus mainly on a client's:",
 "Search for meaning and freedom", ["History of reinforcement", "Unresolved childhood sexual drives", "Faulty family hierarchy"],
 "Existential counseling, associated with Frankl, May and Yalom, deals with meaning, freedom and responsibility, isolation and death."),
("A client keeps coming late and changing the subject when childhood comes up. A psychodynamic counselor would view this as:",
 "Resistance", ["Congruence", "Positive regard", "Generalization"],
 "In psychodynamic theory, resistance is any behavior that keeps painful material out of awareness. The counselor explores it rather than punishing it."),
("A client angry at his boss yells at his children instead. Which defense mechanism is this?",
 "Displacement", ["Projection", "Sublimation", "Rationalization"],
 "Displacement shifts feelings from the real target to a safer one. Sublimation channels impulses into socially valued activity."),
("A client who feels hostile toward a coworker insists that the coworker hates her. Which defense mechanism is this?",
 "Projection", ["Displacement", "Reaction formation", "Denial"],
 "Projection attributes one's own unacceptable feelings to someone else. Reaction formation is acting in the opposite way to a hidden feeling."),
("DBT teaches skills in mindfulness, emotion regulation, interpersonal effectiveness and:",
 "Distress tolerance", ["Dream analysis", "Lifestyle assessment", "Family sculpting"],
 "Linehan's dialectical behavior therapy teaches four skill modules: mindfulness, distress tolerance, emotion regulation and interpersonal effectiveness."),
("In acceptance and commitment therapy, learning to see a thought as just a thought is called:",
 "Cognitive defusion", ["Cognitive restructuring", "Thought stopping", "Free association"],
 "Defusion reduces the grip of thoughts by noticing them rather than arguing with them. Cognitive restructuring, from CBT, changes the content of the thought."),
("The spirit of motivational interviewing is described as partnership, acceptance, compassion and:",
 "Evocation", ["Confrontation", "Persuasion", "Interpretation"],
 "MI spirit draws out the client's own reasons for change (evocation) rather than installing the counselor's. Confrontation and persuasion tend to raise discord."),
("A client says, \"Maybe I should cut back on drinking for my kids.\" In motivational interviewing this statement is:",
 "Change talk", ["Sustain talk", "Discord", "Resistance"],
 "Change talk is the client's own speech in favor of change, such as desire, reasons or need. The counselor draws it out and reflects it."),
("A client pushes back when the counselor urges change. In motivational interviewing, the counselor should:",
 "Roll with it and reflect", ["Argue the case harder", "List the risks again", "Label the client in denial"],
 "Arguing increases discord. MI responds to pushback with reflection, emphasis on choice and shifting focus, sometimes called rolling with resistance."),
("A token economy is based mainly on which theory?",
 "Operant conditioning", ["Classical conditioning", "Psychoanalytic theory", "Existential theory"],
 "A token economy rewards target behaviors with tokens exchanged for backup reinforcers, applying operant conditioning."),
("Taking away a child's tablet time after hitting a sibling, to reduce hitting, is:",
 "Negative punishment", ["Negative reinforcement", "Positive punishment", "Positive reinforcement"],
 "Removing a pleasant stimulus to reduce a behavior is negative punishment. Negative reinforcement removes something unpleasant to increase a behavior."),
# Crisis and safety
("A client in crisis is overwhelmed after a house fire. What is the counselor's first priority?",
 "Ensure safety and basic needs", ["Explore the client's childhood history", "Assign weekly homework", "Give a personality test"],
 "Crisis intervention starts with safety and stabilization, then explores the problem, supports and coping steps. Long-term work waits."),
("A client at moderate suicide risk agrees to outpatient care. What is a recommended step?",
 "Create a written safety plan", ["Have the client sign a no-suicide contract only", "Schedule the next session in a month", "Avoid discussing lethal means"],
 "A safety plan lists warning signs, coping strategies, people and places for distraction, contacts for help and steps to reduce access to lethal means. No-suicide contracts alone are not supported by evidence."),
("A suicidal client keeps a firearm at home. What should the counselor discuss?",
 "Limiting access to the firearm", ["Moving the firearm to the bedroom", "Buying a second firearm", "Nothing, since owning it is legal"],
 "Lethal means counseling, such as storing a firearm off-site or locked away from the client, reduces suicide risk during a crisis."),
("During a telehealth session, what should the counselor confirm at the start?",
 "The client's current location", ["The client's internet speed", "The client's preferred app", "The client's camera brand"],
 "Knowing where the client is makes it possible to send emergency help and to know which state's laws apply."),
("A counselor explains to a client with panic attacks how the body's alarm response works. This is:",
 "Psychoeducation", ["Interpretation", "Confrontation", "Free association"],
 "Psychoeducation gives clients information about their condition and its treatment. Understanding the fight-or-flight response makes panic symptoms less frightening."),
# Family and couples
("A family therapist builds a genogram and works on each member's differentiation of self. This approach is:",
 "Bowen family systems", ["Structural family therapy", "Strategic family therapy", "Experiential therapy"],
 "Bowen's theory centres on differentiation of self, triangles and multigenerational patterns, often mapped with a genogram."),
("Two parents in conflict pull their son into their arguments to take sides. Bowen would call this:",
 "Triangulation", ["Joining", "Enmeshment", "Differentiation"],
 "Triangulation draws a third person into a conflict between two others to reduce the tension between them."),
("A family therapist observes rigid boundaries and maps the family's subsystems and hierarchy. This approach is:",
 "Structural family therapy", ["Narrative therapy", "Bowen family systems", "Solution-focused therapy"],
 "Minuchin's structural family therapy looks at boundaries, subsystems and hierarchy and works to restructure them."),
("A mother and her teenage son are so close that he cannot make decisions without her. In structural terms this is:",
 "Enmeshment", ["Disengagement", "Differentiation", "Homeostasis"],
 "Enmeshment is the result of diffuse boundaries that limit autonomy. Disengagement comes from rigid boundaries that limit closeness."),
("At the start of family therapy, the counselor adapts to the family's style to build trust. Minuchin called this:",
 "Joining", ["Unbalancing", "Reframing", "Detriangling"],
 "Joining is how the structural therapist enters and accommodates to the family system before trying to change it."),
("A strategic family therapist tells a couple to argue on purpose for ten minutes each night. This is a:",
 "Paradoxical directive", ["Genogram exercise", "Miracle question", "Token economy system"],
 "Strategic therapists such as Haley use paradoxical directives, prescribing the symptom so that the family gains control over it or resists it."),
("Virginia Satir's work in family therapy focused especially on:",
 "Communication stances and self-worth", ["Multigenerational transmission", "Schedules of reinforcement and reward", "Unconscious drives and conflicts"],
 "Satir described communication stances such as placating, blaming, super-reasonable and irrelevant, and aimed for congruent communication and self-worth."),
("A family always returns to its familiar patterns after a change. Systems theory calls this tendency:",
 "Homeostasis", ["Equifinality", "Differentiation", "Circular causality"],
 "Homeostasis is a system's tendency to keep balance and resist change. Equifinality means different starting points can lead to the same outcome."),
("A family therapist explores grandparents' influence on the parents' rules. This addresses:",
 "Family-of-origin patterns", ["Token reinforcement plans", "Cognitive distortions", "Group cohesion stages"],
 "Exploring family-of-origin patterns shows how expectations and roles pass down through generations and shape current conflicts."),
# Group work
("In Tuckman's model, which stage comes right after forming?",
 "Storming", ["Norming", "Performing", "Adjourning"],
 "Tuckman's stages are forming, storming, norming, performing and adjourning. Storming is a time of conflict and testing."),
("In which group stage do members most often challenge the leader and resist?",
 "Transition", ["Initial", "Working", "Termination"],
 "In Corey's model, the transition stage is marked by anxiety, defensiveness, conflict and challenges to the leader before the group can move to the working stage."),
("A member keeps telling long stories that take over each session. The leader gently stops him. This skill is:",
 "Blocking", ["Linking", "Drawing out", "Universalizing"],
 "Blocking stops counterproductive behavior such as monopolizing, gossiping or questioning another member aggressively, while protecting members."),
("A leader points out that two members share a fear of rejection. This skill is:",
 "Linking", ["Blocking", "Confronting", "Cutting off"],
 "Linking connects what one member says to another member's concerns, which builds interaction and universality."),
("Before a counseling group starts, why does the leader meet with each candidate?",
 "To screen for fit with the group", ["To collect fees in advance", "To choose a co-leader together for it", "To assign seats and roles"],
 "The ASGW Best Practice Guidelines and the ACA Code ask leaders to screen prospective members so goals fit the group and no one's well-being is put at risk."),
("Which type of group focuses mainly on teaching information and skills, such as anger management?",
 "Psychoeducational group", ["Task group", "Psychotherapy group", "Self-help support group"],
 "Psychoeducational groups teach skills and knowledge with some discussion. Task groups focus on completing work, and psychotherapy groups treat serious problems."),
("Members feel hope from seeing others in the group improve. Which of Yalom's therapeutic factors is this?",
 "Instillation of hope", ["Universality", "Catharsis", "Imitative behavior"],
 "Instillation of hope comes from seeing other members progress. Universality is learning that others share similar problems."),
("A member gains self-esteem from helping another member. Which of Yalom's therapeutic factors is this?",
 "Altruism", ["Imitative behavior", "Universality", "Catharsis"],
 "Altruism is the boost that comes from giving to others in the group."),
("A group admits new members as others leave. This is called a(n):",
 "Open group", ["Closed group", "Task group", "Homogeneous group"],
 "Open groups add members over time. Closed groups keep the same members from start to end, which can build cohesion faster."),
("A leader makes every decision and gives members little input. Lewin would call this style:",
 "Autocratic", ["Democratic", "Laissez-faire", "Facilitative"],
 "Lewin's studies compared autocratic, democratic and laissez-faire leaders. Autocratic leaders direct everything; laissez-faire leaders give little direction."),
("Members keep blaming one quiet member for the group's problems. This is called:",
 "Scapegoating", ["Linking", "Cohesion", "Universality"],
 "Scapegoating places the group's tension on one member. The leader blocks it and helps the group look at its own process."),
("In the final stage of a group, what is a key leader task?",
 "Help members apply their gains", ["Add several new members", "Introduce new conflicts to work on", "Begin deep exploration"],
 "The final stage focuses on consolidating learning, saying goodbye, handling unfinished business and planning to apply gains outside the group."),
("A leader asks, \"What is happening in the group right now?\" This invites:",
 "Process commentary", ["Content review", "Advice giving", "Sharing personal stories"],
 "Process comments turn attention to the here-and-now interactions among members, which Yalom saw as central to interpersonal learning."),
("Two group members begin dating outside the group. How should the leader address it?",
 "Discuss its effect on the group", ["Ignore it completely", "Expel both members from the group at once", "Tell both members' families"],
 "Members' contact outside the group can form subgroups and affect trust. Ground rules address it, and the leader raises its impact openly."),
]
